Truncate in awk's int() with trunc() rather than by conversion to long
long, which is undefined for values out of its range, infinity and NaN.
On x86 such a conversion gives LLONG_MIN, so "int(1e19)", "int(2^63)",
"int(log(0))" and "int(log(-1))" all gave -9223372036854775808. Now
int() leaves an integral value as it is, infinity included, and gives
NaN for NaN, as gawk does. A zero result is kept positive, as the
conversion made it, so "int(-0.5)" still prints and indexes as "0".

---
 editors/awk.c |    4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/awk.c
+++ b/editors/awk.c
@@ -3685,7 +3685,9 @@
 
 			switch (opn) {
 			case F_in:
-				R_d = (long long)L_d;
+				R_d = trunc(L_d);
+				if (R_d == 0)
+					R_d = 0; /* no "-0" */
 				break;
 
 			case F_rn: /*rand*/
